  "preview": "A mini-golf designer needs scale blueprints for three holes — a triangular fairway, a round putting green, and an L-shaped sand trap. One grid square = 4 square feet. Draw them, then explain why area doesn't scale the way length does.",
  "body": {
    "goal": "Translate real measurements into a scale drawing using an *area* scale (1 grid square = N square feet), draw and measure non-rectangular shapes accurately, and discover that linear scale factor and area scale factor are NOT the same.",
    "studentPrompt": "Putter's Paradise is opening a new mini-golf course. The owner hands you graph paper and three real-size measurements. She says: *\"On this paper, every grid square represents 4 square feet of the real course. Draw each hole at scale so I can post the blueprint on the wall.\"*\n\nThe three holes:\n- **Hole 1 (Triangular fairway):** a right triangle with legs of 8 feet and 12 feet.\n- **Hole 2 (Putting green):** a circle with a diameter of 16 feet.\n- **Hole 3 (Sand trap):** an L-shape made by removing a 4 ft × 4 ft square from the corner of an 8 ft × 12 ft rectangle.\n\n1. First, figure out the *linear* scale factor: how many feet of real course does 1 grid-square *length* represent? Show your reasoning — the scale was given in square units, not lengths.\n2. Draw each of the three holes on the grid paper at scale. Label the side lengths in feet.\n3. For each hole, find (a) the real-world area in square feet and (b) the area on the blueprint in grid squares. Check: do (b) × 4 sq ft = (a)?\n4. The owner says, \"If I shrink the whole blueprint so 1 grid square = 1 sq ft, the drawing will be 4 times smaller.\" Is she right? Explain.",
    "image": {
      "alt": "A piece of graph paper sits on a designer's drafting table. Three rough mini-golf hole shapes are sketched on it — a right triangle, a circle, and an L-shape — next to a pencil, a ruler, and a small flag marker.",
      "svg": "<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 480 240\" font-family=\"system-ui, sans-serif\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"480\" height=\"240\" fill=\"#e8d9b8\"/><g transform=\"translate(30,25)\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"420\" height=\"190\" fill=\"#fafaf2\" stroke=\"#5a5a5a\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" rx=\"2\"/><g stroke=\"#9ecae8\" stroke-width=\"0.6\" opacity=\"0.8\"><line x1=\"0\" y1=\"15\" x2=\"420\" y2=\"15\"/><line x1=\"0\" y1=\"30\" x2=\"420\" y2=\"30\"/><line x1=\"0\" y1=\"45\" x2=\"420\" y2=\"45\"/><line x1=\"0\" y1=\"60\" x2=\"420\" y2=\"60\"/><line x1=\"0\" y1=\"75\" x2=\"420\" y2=\"75\"/><line x1=\"0\" y1=\"90\" x2=\"420\" y2=\"90\"/><line x1=\"0\" y1=\"105\" x2=\"420\" y2=\"105\"/><line x1=\"0\" y1=\"120\" x2=\"420\" y2=\"120\"/><line x1=\"0\" y1=\"135\" x2=\"420\" y2=\"135\"/><line x1=\"0\" y1=\"150\" x2=\"420\" y2=\"150\"/><line x1=\"0\" y1=\"165\" x2=\"420\" y2=\"165\"/><line x1=\"0\" y1=\"180\" x2=\"420\" y2=\"180\"/><line x1=\"15\" y1=\"0\" x2=\"15\" y2=\"190\"/><line x1=\"30\" y1=\"0\" x2=\"30\" y2=\"190\"/><line x1=\"45\" y1=\"0\" x2=\"45\" y2=\"190\"/><line x1=\"60\" y1=\"0\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"190\"/><line x1=\"75\" y1=\"0\" x2=\"75\" y2=\"190\"/><line x1=\"90\" y1=\"0\" x2=\"90\" y2=\"190\"/><line x1=\"105\" y1=\"0\" x2=\"105\" y2=\"190\"/><line x1=\"120\" y1=\"0\" x2=\"120\" y2=\"190\"/><line x1=\"135\" y1=\"0\" x2=\"135\" y2=\"190\"/><line x1=\"150\" y1=\"0\" x2=\"150\" y2=\"190\"/><line x1=\"165\" y1=\"0\" x2=\"165\" y2=\"190\"/><line x1=\"180\" y1=\"0\" x2=\"180\" y2=\"190\"/><line x1=\"195\" y1=\"0\" x2=\"195\" y2=\"190\"/><line x1=\"210\" y1=\"0\" x2=\"210\" y2=\"190\"/><line x1=\"225\" y1=\"0\" x2=\"225\" y2=\"190\"/><line x1=\"240\" y1=\"0\" x2=\"240\" y2=\"190\"/><line x1=\"255\" y1=\"0\" x2=\"255\" y2=\"190\"/><line x1=\"270\" y1=\"0\" x2=\"270\" y2=\"190\"/><line x1=\"285\" y1=\"0\" x2=\"285\" y2=\"190\"/><line x1=\"300\" y1=\"0\" x2=\"300\" y2=\"190\"/><line x1=\"315\" y1=\"0\" x2=\"315\" y2=\"190\"/><line x1=\"330\" y1=\"0\" x2=\"330\" y2=\"190\"/><line x1=\"345\" y1=\"0\" x2=\"345\" y2=\"190\"/><line x1=\"360\" y1=\"0\" x2=\"360\" y2=\"190\"/><line x1=\"375\" y1=\"0\" x2=\"375\" y2=\"190\"/><line x1=\"390\" y1=\"0\" x2=\"390\" y2=\"190\"/><line x1=\"405\" y1=\"0\" x2=\"405\" y2=\"190\"/></g><polygon points=\"25,140 25,80 85,140\" fill=\"#9bd1a7\" fill-opacity=\"0.55\" stroke=\"#2f6b3e\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><circle cx=\"195\" cy=\"110\" r=\"40\" fill=\"#cbe7f7\" fill-opacity=\"0.55\" stroke=\"#1f5d8f\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><circle cx=\"195\" cy=\"110\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#1f5d8f\"/><path d=\"M300,140 L300,80 L360,80 L360,110 L330,110 L330,140 Z\" fill=\"#f1d28a\" fill-opacity=\"0.6\" stroke=\"#9c6b1d\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><g font-size=\"9\" fill=\"#333\" font-weight=\"600\"><text x=\"55\" y=\"160\">Hole 1</text><text x=\"175\" y=\"170\">Hole 2</text><text x=\"320\" y=\"160\">Hole 3</text></g><line x1=\"195\" y1=\"110\" x2=\"203\" y2=\"100\" stroke=\"#1f5d8f\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/><polygon points=\"203,100 215,103 205,108\" fill=\"#d93b3b\"/></g><g transform=\"translate(355,205)\" fill=\"#888\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"90\" height=\"6\" rx=\"1\" fill=\"#c9a86a\"/><g stroke=\"#6a5126\" stroke-width=\"0.6\"><line x1=\"10\" y1=\"0\" x2=\"10\" y2=\"6\"/><line x1=\"25\" y1=\"0\" x2=\"25\" y2=\"6\"/><line x1=\"40\" y1=\"0\" x2=\"40\" y2=\"6\"/><line x1=\"55\" y1=\"0\" x2=\"55\" y2=\"6\"/><line x1=\"70\" y1=\"0\" x2=\"70\" y2=\"6\"/></g></g><g transform=\"translate(20,210)\"><polygon points=\"0,0 70,0 78,8 70,16 0,16\" fill=\"#f4d35e\" stroke=\"#7a5b14\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/><circle cx=\"76\" cy=\"8\" r=\"1.5\" fill=\"#333\"/></g></svg>"
    },
    "launch": "Hand each group a half-sheet of quarter-inch graph paper and a ruler. Tell the story: a designer needs blueprints. Read the scale aloud — \"one grid square equals four square feet\" — and then ask, \"Wait... what does that mean for the LENGTH of one side of a grid square?\" Don't answer it. Send them to the boards (or tables) and let groups figure out what the scale means before they draw.",
    "materials": [
      "Graph paper (quarter-inch or centimeter grid)",
      "Rulers",
      "Pencils with erasers",
      "Compass or round object for the circle (optional)",
      "Vertical whiteboards for Q1 reasoning and Q4 discussion"
    ],
    "concepts": [
      "scale-factor",
      "scale-drawing",
      "area-vs-length-scaling",
      "square-root",
      "composite-area",
      "non-rectangular-shapes"
    ],
    "taskType": "performance-task",
    "btcFit": "Strong performance task with a low-floor entry (Q1 is a puzzle — but anyone can guess and check). Q4 is the diamond: the owner's claim sounds reasonable, but she's wrong by a factor of 2 (linear shrink is √4 = 2, not 4). The circle and L-shape force students past simple rectangle scaling — the same conceptual move the source PowerPoint asks for in its 'make your own shape' part.",
    "anticipatedApproaches": "- Q1: Some groups will jump to '1 grid square = 4 feet on each side' (wrong — that gives 16 sq ft per grid square). Strong groups reason: if 1 sq grid square = 4 sq ft, then the SIDE of one grid square is √4 = 2 ft. So linear scale is 1 grid unit = 2 feet.\n- Q2 (Hole 1, triangle): legs of 8 ft and 12 ft → 4 grid squares and 6 grid squares. Right triangle.\n- Q2 (Hole 2, circle): diameter 16 ft → 8 grid squares. Radius = 4 grid squares.\n- Q2 (Hole 3, L-shape): an 8 ft × 12 ft rectangle = 4 × 6 grid squares, with a 4 ft × 4 ft square (2 × 2 grid squares) removed from one corner.\n- Q3: Real areas — Triangle: ½(8)(12) = 48 sq ft. Circle: π(8)² ≈ 201 sq ft. L-shape: 8×12 − 4×4 = 96 − 16 = 80 sq ft. Blueprint areas (in grid squares): Triangle ½(4)(6) = 12; Circle π(4)² ≈ 50.3; L-shape 4×6 − 2×2 = 20. Check: 12 × 4 = 48 ✓; 50.3 × 4 ≈ 201 ✓; 20 × 4 = 80 ✓.\n- Q4: The owner is WRONG. Shrinking AREA by a factor of 4 shrinks each LENGTH by a factor of 2 (not 4). The drawing will be 2 times smaller (in each direction), and its total area on the page will be 4× smaller.",
    "commonMisconceptions": "- 'If 1 sq unit = 4 sq ft, then 1 unit length = 4 ft.' This conflates area and length. Push them to draw one grid square and label its side and area separately.\n- Drawing the circle's RADIUS as 16 grid squares (using the diameter directly). Catch with: how big is your circle in real feet?\n- Computing the L-shape area by adding instead of subtracting (or trying to use a non-existent 'L-shape formula').\n- For Q4, agreeing with the owner because '4 sq ft → 1 sq ft is a factor of 4 in area, so lengths shrink by 4 too.' This is the SAME misconception that drove Business Card Scaling Q4, just in reverse. Use a 2×2 vs. 1×1 square as a counter-example: area drops from 4 to 1 (×¼), side drops from 2 to 1 (×½).",
    "discussionQuestions": [
      "How did you decide what one grid-square LENGTH represents? Some groups said 4 feet, some said 2 feet. Both groups got the same answer for the area scale — what's the difference?",
      "The circle's blueprint area was about 50 grid squares, but you can't really count 50 grid squares inside the circle (some are partial). How did you handle that?",
      "Walk us through Hole 3. Why subtract instead of measure all the way around?",
      "The owner's claim in Q4 sounds right at first. What exactly is she confusing?"
    ],
    "extensions": [
      "The designer changes the scale to 1 grid square = 9 sq ft and asks for a SECOND blueprint of the same three holes. What is the new linear scale factor? Re-draw Hole 1 (the triangle) at the new scale. How does the new blueprint area compare to the old one — for the same real-world hole?",
      "Design your own fourth hole. It can't be a triangle, circle, or rectangle. Give the real-world dimensions in feet, draw it at the original scale (1 grid square = 4 sq ft), and find its real-world area two ways: (a) compute from the real measurements, (b) count grid squares on your blueprint and multiply by 4. The two answers should agree within rounding.",
      "The whole 18-hole course will fit on a poster where 1 grid square = 25 sq ft. If a real fairway has area 400 sq ft, what is its area on the poster (in grid squares)? Now generalize: write a rule connecting the AREA scale factor (k) to the LINEAR scale factor. Test your rule against this task (k = 4) and the poster (k = 25)."
    ],
    "sampleSolutions": "**Q1.** If 1 sq grid square represents 4 sq ft, then one grid square has side √4 = 2 ft. Linear scale: 1 grid unit length = 2 feet.\n\n**Q2.**\n- Hole 1 (right triangle): legs of 4 grid units (= 8 ft) and 6 grid units (= 12 ft).\n- Hole 2 (circle): diameter 8 grid units (= 16 ft), radius 4 grid units.\n- Hole 3 (L-shape): 4 × 6 grid rectangle with a 2 × 2 grid square removed from a corner.\n\n**Q3.**\n| Hole | Real area (sq ft) | Blueprint area (grid sq) | Check: blueprint × 4 |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 Triangle | ½(8)(12) = 48 | ½(4)(6) = 12 | 12 × 4 = 48 ✓ |\n| 2 Circle | π(8)² ≈ 201.1 | π(4)² ≈ 50.3 | 50.3 × 4 ≈ 201 ✓ |\n| 3 L-shape | 8·12 − 4·4 = 80 | 4·6 − 2·2 = 20 | 20 × 4 = 80 ✓ |\n\n**Q4.** The owner is wrong. The AREA scale changes by a factor of 4 (each grid square shrinks from 4 sq ft to 1 sq ft), but each LENGTH only changes by a factor of √4 = 2. So the drawing will be half as long and half as wide, not a quarter as long. Its total area on the page IS 4 times smaller, which is probably what she meant — but the drawing itself is only 2× smaller in each direction.\n\nGeneral rule: if 1 grid square = k sq ft, then 1 grid unit length = √k ft.",
